23:13:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: We’ve essentially already run out of new, publicly routable IPv4 addresses. The global pool was exhausted by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) in 2011, and each Regional Internet Registry (RIR) has been exhausting its own allocations ever since (e.g., APNIC in 2011, RIPE NCC in 2019, ARIN in 2015). So “what actually happens” is not a single catastrophic event, but a series of ongoing consequences, workarounds, and gradual transitions. ### Immediate, practical consequences: 1. **No new “new” public IPv4 addresses** – If you start a brand-new ISP, data center, or large network, you can no longer get a fresh block of public IPv4 addresses from your RIR. You have to buy them on the secondary market (at prices that can be $30–$50 per address) or use workarounds. 2. **Widespread Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)** – Internet Service Providers now often place thousands of customers behind a single public IPv4 address using large-scale NAT (like CG-NAT, also called “100.64.0.0/10” addressing). This means customers don’t get a unique public IP and can’t easily host servers or use peer-to-peer applications without extra effort (e.g., using port forwarding, UPnP, or tunneling). 3. **IPv4 address trading and leasing** – A secondary market has emerged where organizations buy/sell/lease existing IPv4 blocks. This has driven up costs for companies that need public IPs (e.g., for hosting, VPNs, IoT devices). 4. **Fragmentation of the internet** – Because many ISPs use CGNAT, the end-to-end principle of the internet is weakened. Some applications (online gaming, VoIP, certain video calls) may suffer from NAT traversal issues. Also, logging for law enforcement becomes more complicated since many users share one IP. 5. **IPv6 deployment accelerates** – The lack of IPv4 is the main driver for IPv6 adoption. More ISPs, mobile networks, and content providers (like Google, Facebook, Netflix) now serve over IPv6 to their users. As of 2025, global IPv6 adoption is around 40–50% of internet traffic. Devices, routers, and operating systems are increasingly dual-stack. 6. **“Sharing” becomes the norm** – Beyond CGNAT, techniques like DS-Lite (Dual-Stack Lite), MAP-T, and 464XLAT allow carriers to give customers an IPv6-only connection while still providing access to IPv4-only services via translation. ### What does NOT happen? - **The internet doesn’t break** – Existing IPv4 addresses still work, and connections between IPv4-only networks continue normally. - **You don’t lose your current IP address** – No one takes away addresses already allocated. - **Private IPv4 addresses (10.x.x.x, 192.168.x.x, 172.16-31.x.x) remain** – They are endlessly reusable for internal networks; the shortage only affects public, routable addresses. ### Long-term outlook - **IPv4 will remain usable** but become a scarce, expensive commodity. Many networks will be “IPv4 as a service” tunnels over IPv6. - **The internet will become increasingly dual-stack or IPv6-only** with translation layers. Eventually, when IPv4 is too expensive and inconvenient, more services will deprecate it. - **Some regions** (Africa, Latin America) that got IPv4 allocation later are even more constrained and rely heavily on CGNAT and IPv6 from the start. In short: running out of IPv4 addresses **already happened** in a gradual way. The result is not a shutdown but a patchwork of NAT, address trading, and a slower-than-ideal migration to IPv6.
