23:14:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely correct. All the workarounds—CGNAT, address trading, carrier-grade translations—do **not** create a single new IPv4 address. They simply squeeze more usage out of the existing, finite pool of about 4.3 billion addresses. That’s the fundamental limit: IPv4 is a 32-bit number, and you cannot magically expand it. Here’s a clearer breakdown of what each workaround actually does: ### 1. CGNAT (Carrier-Grade NAT) - **What it does:** Instead of giving each customer a unique public IPv4, the ISP puts hundreds or thousands of customers behind a single public IP. - **Result:** The same IPv4 address is reused for many users simultaneously. This is **not** multiplication; it’s time-sharing. The address is reused, but it still can only handle a limited number of simultaneous connections per user (port limit). ### 2. Address Trading / Leasing - **What it does:** Organizations that have unused IPv4 blocks sell or rent them to those who need more. - **Result:** This is a zero-sum game. Addresses move from one user to another, but the total number of public IPv4s in existence remains constant. No new addresses are created. ### 3. IPv4-as-a-Service (over IPv6) - **What it does:** The ISP gives you an IPv6-only connection, but uses translation (like 464XLAT or NAT64) to let you reach IPv4-only websites. - **Result:** You don’t get your own public IPv4 address at all. The ISP pools many users onto a small set of shared IPv4 addresses (often via CGNAT). Again, no new IPv4 addresses are born. ### 4. Private IPv4 (RFC 1918) + NAT at home - **What it does:** Your home router uses a private IP (192.168.x.x) internally and maps it to your single public IPv4 via NAT. - **Result:** You already know this: one public IP serves many devices. This is the same principle as CGNAT, just at a smaller scale. --- ### So why bother if it’s just reusing? These workarounds are **temporary measures** to delay the pain of IPv4 exhaustion. Without them, many new users would have no public IPv4 at all, and the internet would fragment. With CGNAT, for example, a new ISP can still serve tens of thousands of customers using only a few hundred public IPs. The trade-offs are: - **Loss of end-to-end connectivity:** Applications that need direct IP access (hosting servers, peer-to-peer gaming, IoT control) break or require complex tunneling. - **Performance issues:** NAT introduces latency, and many users sharing one IP can hit port limits. - **No new growth:** Even with CGNAT, the number of public IPv4 addresses is capped. As more devices come online (IoT, 5G, smart cities), reuse alone won’t keep up. --- ### The only real solution: IPv6 IPv6 (128-bit addresses) creates **340 undecillion** unique addresses—enough for every grain of sand on Earth many times over. That’s the only way to *add* new addresses to the global pool. All the IPv4 workarounds are just buying time until the world fully adopts IPv6. Unfortunately, the transition has been slow because: - IPv4 works “well enough” with NAT for most home users. - IPv6 requires new hardware, software, and expertise. - Content providers still need to support IPv4-only users. So you’ve hit the nail on the head: we are stuck with a fixed resource, and all we can do is share it more cleverly. The real answer to IPv4 exhaustion is not to find more IPv4 addresses—it’s to move to a system that has enough from the start.
