rning back into liquid**. The actual vaporization (liquid → gas) happens invisibly inside the kettle, and also invisibly at room temperature — it's just slower and less concentrated. ## 4. The key takeaway - **Boiling** at 100°C produces enough invisible vapor that, when it hits cool air, forms a visible white cloud. - **Evaporation** at room temperature produces invisible vapor so slowly that it never makes a visible cloud. You only *see* the effect of vaporization when it's fast and concentrated enough to condense into visible droplets. But the process of liquid turning into invisible gas happens all the time, even on a cool day — you just can't see it. I hope that clears up the difference! If you'd like, I can explain why the droplets form a cloud instead of just falling.
